@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.998 = private unnamed_addr constant [1203 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for calls to `marshal` functions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ADeserializing untrusted data with `marshal` is insecure, as it can allow for\0Athe creation of arbitrary objects, which can then be used to achieve\0Aarbitrary code execution and otherwise unexpected behavior.\0A\0AAvoid deserializing untrusted data with `marshal`. Instead, consider safer\0Aformats, such as JSON.\0A\0AIf you must deserialize untrusted data with `marshal`, consider signing the\0Adata with a secret key and verifying the signature before deserializing the\0Apayload. This will prevent an attacker from injecting arbitrary objects\0Ainto the serialized data.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to `marshal` functions.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport marshal\0A\0Awith open(\22foo.marshal\22, \22rb\22) as file:\0A    foo = marshal.load(file)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport json\0A\0Awith open(\22foo.json\22, \22rb\22) as file:\0A    foo = json.load(file)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `marshal` \E2\80\94 Internal Python object serialization](https://docs.python.org/3/library/marshal.html)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-502](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
