@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1022 = private unnamed_addr constant [1611 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of calls to `django.utils.safestring.mark_safe`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ACross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities allow attackers to execute\0Aarbitrary JavaScript. To guard against XSS attacks, Django templates\0Aassumes that data is unsafe and automatically escapes malicious strings\0Abefore rending them.\0A\0A`django.utils.safestring.mark_safe` marks a string as safe for use in HTML\0Atemplates, bypassing XSS protection. Its usage can be dangerous if the\0Acontents of the string are dynamically generated, because it may allow\0Across-site scripting attacks if the string is not properly escaped.\0A\0AFor dynamically generated strings, consider utilizing\0A`django.utils.html.format_html`.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to `django.utils.safestring.mark_safe`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom django.utils.safestring import mark_safe\0A\0A\0Adef render_username(username):\0A    return mark_safe(f\22<i>{username}</i>\22)  # Dangerous if username is user-provided.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom django.utils.html import format_html\0A\0A\0Adef render_username(username):\0A    return django.utils.html.format_html(\22<i>{}</i>\22, username)  # username is escaped.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Django documentation: `mark_safe`](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/utils/#django.utils.safestring.mark_safe)\0A- [Django documentation: Cross Site Scripting (XSS) protection](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/topics/security/#cross-site-scripting-xss-protection)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-80](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/80.html)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
