@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1025 = private unnamed_addr constant [25 x i8] c"suspicious-url-open-usage", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1026 = private unnamed_addr constant [1155 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for instances where URL open functions are used with unexpected schemes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASome URL open functions allow the use of `file:` or custom schemes (for use\0Ainstead of `http:` or `https:`). An attacker may be able to use these\0Aschemes to access or modify unauthorized resources, and cause unexpected\0Abehavior.\0A\0ATo mitigate this risk, audit all uses of URL open functions and ensure that\0Aonly permitted schemes are used (e.g., allowing `http:` and `https:`, and\0Adisallowing `file:` and `ftp:`).\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to URL open functions.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom urllib.request import urlopen\0A\0Aurl = input(\22Enter a URL: \22)\0A\0Awith urlopen(url) as response:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom urllib.request import urlopen\0A\0Aurl = input(\22Enter a URL: \22)\0A\0Aif not url.startswith((\22http:\22, \22https:\22)):\0A    raise ValueError(\22URL must start with 'http:' or 'https:'\22)\0A\0Awith urlopen(url) as response:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `urlopen`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/urllib.request.html#urllib.request.urlopen)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
