@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1054 = private unnamed_addr constant [1210 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `ssl._create_unverified_context`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 476] enabled certificate and hostname validation by default in Python\0Astandard library HTTP clients. Previously, Python did not validate\0Acertificates by default, which could allow an attacker to perform a \22man in\0Athe middle\22 attack by intercepting and modifying traffic between client and\0Aserver.\0A\0ATo support legacy environments, `ssl._create_unverified_context` reverts to\0Athe previous behavior that does perform verification. Otherwise, use\0A`ssl.create_default_context` to create a secure context.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to `ssl._create_unverified_context`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport ssl\0A\0Acontext = ssl._create_unverified_context()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport ssl\0A\0Acontext = ssl.create_default_context()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 476 \E2\80\93 Enabling certificate verification by default for stdlib http clients: Opting out](https://peps.python.org/pep-0476/#opting-out)\0A- [Python documentation: `ssl` \E2\80\94 TLS/SSL wrapper for socket objects](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html)\0A\0A[PEP 476]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0476/\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
