@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1120 = private unnamed_addr constant [1001 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `str.format` calls in `gettext` function calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn the `gettext` API, the `gettext` function (often aliased to `_`) returns\0Aa translation of its input argument by looking it up in a translation\0Acatalog.\0A\0ACalling `gettext` with a formatted string as its argument can cause\0Aunexpected behavior. Since the formatted string is resolved before the\0Afunction call, the translation catalog will look up the formatted string,\0Arather than the `str.format`-style template.\0A\0AInstead, format the value returned by the function call, rather than\0Aits argument.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom gettext import gettext as _\0A\0Aname = \22Maria\22\0A_(\22Hello, {}!\22.format(name))  # Looks for \22Hello, Maria!\22.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom gettext import gettext as _\0A\0Aname = \22Maria\22\0A_(\22Hello, %s!\22) % name  # Looks for \22Hello, %s!\22.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `gettext` \E2\80\94 Multilingual internationalization services](https://docs.python.org/3/library/gettext.html)\0A", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1121 = private unnamed_addr constant [90 x i8] c"`format` method argument is resolved before function call; consider `_(\22string %s\22) % arg`", align 1
