@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1150 = private unnamed_addr constant [1806 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `logging.error` that pass `exc_info=True`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ACalling `logging.error` with `exc_info=True` is equivalent to calling\0A`logging.exception`. Using `logging.exception` is more concise, more\0Areadable, and conveys the intent of the logging statement more clearly.\0A\0A## Known problems\0A\0AThis rule detects uses of the `logging` module via a heuristic.\0ASpecifically, it matches against:\0A\0A- Uses of the `logging` module itself (e.g., `import logging; logging.info(...)`).\0A- Uses of `flask.current_app.logger` (e.g., `from flask import current_app; current_app.logger.info(...)`).\0A- Objects whose name starts with `log` or ends with `logger` or `logging`,\0A  when used in the same file in which they are defined (e.g., `logger = logging.getLogger(); logger.info(...)`).\0A- Imported objects marked as loggers via the [`lint.logger-objects`] setting, which can be\0A  used to enforce these rules against shared logger objects (e.g., `from module import logger; logger.info(...)`,\0A  when [`lint.logger-objects`] is set to `[\22module.logger\22]`).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept ValueError:\0A    logging.error(\22Exception occurred\22, exc_info=True)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept ValueError:\0A    logging.exception(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.logger-objects`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `logging.exception`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logging.exception)\0A- [Python documentation: `exception`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logging.Logger.exception)\0A- [Python documentation: `logging.error`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logging.error)\0A- [Python documentation: `error`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logging.Logger.error)\0A", align 1
