@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1167 = private unnamed_addr constant [1517 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for simple `__iter__` methods that return `Generator`, and for\0Asimple `__aiter__` methods that return `AsyncGenerator`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing `(Async)Iterator` for these methods is simpler and more elegant. More\0Aimportantly, it also reflects the fact that the precise kind of iterator\0Areturned from an `__iter__` method is usually an implementation detail that\0Acould change at any time. Type annotations help define a contract for a\0Afunction; implementation details should not leak into that contract.\0A\0AFor example:\0A```python\0Afrom collections.abc import AsyncGenerator, Generator\0Afrom typing import Any\0A\0A\0Aclass CustomIterator:\0A    def __iter__(self) -> Generator:\0A        yield from range(42)\0A\0A\0Aclass CustomIterator2:\0A    def __iter__(self) -> Generator[str, Any, None]:\0A        yield from \22abcdefg\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom collections.abc import Iterator\0A\0A\0Aclass CustomIterator:\0A    def __iter__(self) -> Iterator:\0A        yield from range(42)\0A\0A\0Aclass CustomIterator2:\0A    def __iter__(self) -> Iterator[str]:\0A        yield from \22abdefg\22\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule tries hard to avoid false-positive errors, and the rule's fix\0Ashould always be safe for `.pyi` stub files. However, there is a slightly\0Ahigher chance that a false positive might be emitted by this rule when\0Aapplied to runtime Python (`.py` files). As such, the fix is marked as\0Aunsafe for any `__iter__` or `__aiter__` method in a `.py` file that has\0Amore than two statements (including docstrings) in its body.\0A", align 1
