@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1198 = private unnamed_addr constant [1054 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `if` statements that can be replaced with `bool`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`if` statements that return `True` for a truthy condition and `False` for\0Aa falsy condition can be replaced with boolean casts.\0A\0A## Example\0AGiven:\0A```python\0Adef foo(x: int) -> bool:\0A    if x > 0:\0A        return True\0A    else:\0A        return False\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef foo(x: int) -> bool:\0A    return x > 0\0A```\0A\0AOr, given:\0A```python\0Adef foo(x: int) -> bool:\0A    if x > 0:\0A        return True\0A    return False\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef foo(x: int) -> bool:\0A    return x > 0\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is marked as unsafe because it may change the program\E2\80\99s behavior if the condition does not\0Areturn a proper Boolean. While the fix will try to wrap non-boolean values in a call to bool,\0Acustom implementations of comparison functions like `__eq__` can avoid the bool call and still\0Alead to altered behavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Truth Value Testing](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#truth-value-testing)\0A", align 1
