@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1206 = private unnamed_addr constant [26 x i8] c"no-slots-in-tuple-subclass", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1207 = private unnamed_addr constant [1060 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for subclasses of `tuple` that lack a `__slots__` definition.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, the `__slots__` attribute allows you to explicitly define the\0Aattributes (instance variables) that a class can have. By default, Python\0Auses a dictionary to store an object's attributes, which incurs some memory\0Aoverhead. However, when `__slots__` is defined, Python uses a more compact\0Ainternal structure to store the object's attributes, resulting in memory\0Asavings.\0A\0ASubclasses of `tuple` inherit all the attributes and methods of the\0Abuilt-in `tuple` class. Since tuples are typically immutable, they don't\0Arequire additional attributes beyond what the `tuple` class provides.\0ADefining `__slots__` for subclasses of `tuple` prevents the creation of a\0Adictionary for each instance, reducing memory consumption.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Foo(tuple):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Foo(tuple):\0A    __slots__ = ()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `__slots__`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#slots)\0A", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1208 = private unnamed_addr constant [47 x i8] c"Subclasses of `tuple` should define `__slots__`", align 1
