@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1342 = private unnamed_addr constant [35 x i8] c"unexpected-special-method-signature", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1343 = private unnamed_addr constant [988 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for \22special\22 methods that have an unexpected method signature.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A\22Special\22 methods, like `__len__`, are expected to adhere to a specific,\0Astandard function signature. Implementing a \22special\22 method using a\0Anon-standard function signature can lead to unexpected and surprising\0Abehavior for users of a given class.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Bookshelf:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self._books = [\22Foo\22, \22Bar\22, \22Baz\22]\0A\0A    def __len__(self, index):  # __len__ does not except an index parameter\0A        return len(self._books)\0A\0A    def __getitem__(self, index):\0A        return self._books[index]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Bookshelf:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self._books = [\22Foo\22, \22Bar\22, \22Baz\22]\0A\0A    def __len__(self):\0A        return len(self._books)\0A\0A    def __getitem__(self, index):\0A        return self._books[index]\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Data model](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html)\0A", align 1
