@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1353 = private unnamed_addr constant [1478 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `open` and related calls without an explicit `encoding`\0Aargument.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing `open` in text mode without an explicit encoding can lead to\0Anon-portable code, with differing behavior across platforms. While readers\0Amay assume that UTF-8 is the default encoding, in reality, the default\0Ais locale-specific.\0A\0AInstead, consider using the `encoding` parameter to enforce a specific\0Aencoding. [PEP 597] recommends the use of `encoding=\22utf-8\22` as a default,\0Aand suggests that it may become the default in future versions of Python.\0A\0AIf a locale-specific encoding is intended, use `encoding=\22locale\22`  on\0APython 3.10 and later, or `locale.getpreferredencoding()` on earlier versions,\0Ato make the encoding explicit.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is always unsafe and may change the program's behavior. It forces\0A`encoding=\22utf-8\22` as the default, regardless of the platform\E2\80\99s actual default\0Aencoding, which may cause `UnicodeDecodeError` on non-UTF-8 systems.\0A```python\0Awith open(\22test.txt\22) as f:\0A    print(f.read()) # before fix (on UTF-8 systems): \E4\BD\A0\E5\A5\BD\EF\BC\8C\E4\B8\96\E7\95\8C\EF\BC\81\0Awith open(\22test.txt\22, encoding=\22utf-8\22) as f:\0A    print(f.read()) # after fix (on Windows): UnicodeDecodeError\0A```\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aopen(\22file.txt\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aopen(\22file.txt\22, encoding=\22utf-8\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `open`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#open)\0A\0A[PEP 597]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0597/\0A", align 1
