@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1379 = private unnamed_addr constant [1524 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `datetime.fromisoformat()` calls\0Awhere the only argument is an inline replacement\0Aof `Z` with a zero offset timezone.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AOn Python 3.11 and later, `datetime.fromisoformat()` can handle most [ISO 8601][iso-8601]\0Aformats including ones affixed with `Z`, so such an operation is unnecessary.\0A\0AMore information on unsupported formats\0Acan be found in [the official documentation][fromisoformat].\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom datetime import datetime\0A\0A\0Adate = \222025-01-01T00:00:00Z\22\0A\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(date.replace(\22Z\22, \22+00:00\22))\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(date[:-1] + \22-00\22)\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(date.strip(\22Z\22, \22-0000\22))\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(date.rstrip(\22Z\22, \22-00:00\22))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom datetime import datetime\0A\0A\0Adate = \222025-01-01T00:00:00Z\22\0A\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(date)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix is always marked as unsafe,\0Aas it might change the program's behaviour.\0A\0AFor example, working code might become non-working:\0A\0A```python\0Ad = \22Z2025-01-01T00:00:00Z\22  # Note the leading `Z`\0A\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(d.strip(\22Z\22) + \22+00:00\22)  # Fine\0Adatetime.fromisoformat(d)  # Runtime error\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A* [What\E2\80\99s New In Python 3.11 &sect; `datetime`](https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.11.html#datetime)\0A* [`fromisoformat`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html#datetime.date.fromisoformat)\0A\0A[iso-8601]: https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/#iso:std:iso:8601\0A[fromisoformat]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html#datetime.date.fromisoformat\0A", align 1
