@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1383 = private unnamed_addr constant [1461 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `enumerate` that discard either the index or the value\0Awhen iterating over a sequence.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe built-in `enumerate` function is useful when you need both the index and\0Avalue of a sequence.\0A\0AIf you only need the index or values of a sequence, you should iterate over\0A`range(len(...))` or the sequence itself, respectively, instead. This is\0Amore efficient and communicates the intent of the code more clearly.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule is prone to false negatives due to type inference limitations;\0Anamely, it will only suggest a fix using the `len` builtin function if the\0Asequence passed to `enumerate` is an instantiated as a list, set, dict, or\0Atuple literal, or annotated as such with a type annotation.\0A\0AThe `len` builtin function is not defined for all object types (such as\0Agenerators), and so refactoring to use `len` over `enumerate` is not always\0Asafe.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afor index, _ in enumerate(sequence):\0A    print(index)\0A\0Afor _, value in enumerate(sequence):\0A    print(value)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afor index in range(len(sequence)):\0A    print(index)\0A\0Afor value in sequence:\0A    print(value)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `enumerate`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#enumerate)\0A- [Python documentation: `range`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#range)\0A- [Python documentation: `len`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#len)\0A", align 1
